From 804b3cb3c4ae4a7598a23eac67927fdca0aa7d5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: arm64: keep translations across launches in a
 per-app cache

Step P2 of the persistent translation cache (ANAL-381), on patches 10 and
11. Translations of file-backed guest code are written to the app's
code_cache and installed on a later launch instead of being translated
again. Off by default: berberis.pcache (BERBERIS_PCACHE) = off | on | verify.

- Loading is lazy, in TranslateRegion's first gear, after
  AddAndLockForTranslation. A record is installed only if, in this order:
  the file's header matches this translator (build-id) and its settings
  (mode, gear-up threshold, flags, host CPU features, page sizes); the
  record's private copy has a good CRC32C and in-bounds lengths and
  operands; [pc, pc + size) is executable and still in the mapping the
  record was found through; and the guest bytes equal the bytes the
  translation was made from. Then it's re-linked into the code pool
  (CodePool::AddPrebuilt) and published like a translation, so an
  invalidation during the load discards it. Regions start in the tier they
  ended on: heavy, lite with its counter (re-linked to the new entry's), or
  lite settled after a declined gear-up.
- A mapping registry (GuestMappingObserver, set by the cache) follows every
  guest mmap, munmap, mprotect and mremap under an exclusive lock around
  the host call. An executable file mapping is attributed to its ELF image
  (GNU build-id, or dev/ino/size/mtime, plus the ELF's file offset) by
  checking its program headers against the mappings. Checks, the byte
  compare, install and publication hold the shared lock, so the range
  can't be unmapped, re-protected or replaced underneath them. A changed
  mapping loses its attribution: a conservative miss.
- MmapForGuest invalidates the translations of executable code replaced by
  an executable MAP_FIXED mapping, which keeps the shadow's bits and
  invalidated nothing before.
- The translators capture the instruction words they decode (decoded_insns
  in the lite and heavy params). A translation is symbolized after install
  and queued after publication if the mapping is the one it started in.
- Writing: one writer per module, by flock on a lock file, holding it on a
  host-owned fd checked before each write. Files are created by rename and
  appended to: records, and an index log read at open. Queued records are
  flushed by a translating thread at 1 MiB or 2 s after the last flush, at
  exit and at the guest's exit_group (g_guest_exit_hooks). No thread is
  started. 128 MiB cap per app, checked against the directory's size at
  each flush, so it counts what the app's other processes wrote.
- A fork child stops using the cache without taking a lock (pthread_atfork,
  and g_guest_fork_child_hooks from the guest's raw-clone fork): it drops
  the mapping observer and closes its copies of the writer lock fds.
- verify: the candidate is installed only if its code and recovery points,
  re-linked to one address, equal a fresh translation with its tier,
  parameters and end. Otherwise the fresh one is installed, and a second
  fresh translation tells a mismatch (the two agree) from inconclusive.
  Both fresh translations read guest memory, so they, and the install and
  publication of whichever code is chosen, run under the same shared hold
  of the mapping lock as the checks.
- Counters pcache_* in translator_stats; loaded regions are lite_cached and
  heavy_cached in the perf map. The final stats line's buffer grows to fit
  them; on the host it goes to stderr directly, past liblog's 1024 bytes.

Tests: persistent_cache_test.cc (24): loads in each tier in a new
"launch" at another address, verify match; each refusal and their order;
torn and corrupt files; executable replacement; protection removal, remap
and replacement before the check; an unmap racing the comparison; an
invalidation during the load; the counter re-link; flush points; one
writer across fork, an independent process and the guest's raw clone with
the mapping lock held; verify's deterministic mismatch, nondeterminism and
recovery-only mismatch, each checking the code installed; protection
taken away and an unmap during verify's two translations, the second on
the way to installing fresh code; the cap counting another process's
files.
PersistentCacheBench.DISABLED_ReplayCorpus replays a region corpus
through write, load and verify launches.

